The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
ifs_alloc(iter->inode, folio, iter->flags);
if (*bytes_submitted == folio_len)
    ctx->cur_folio = NULL;
```
Reason:  
`ifs_alloc` may not attach an `iomap_folio_state` (ifs) when `1 << inode->i_blkbits` matches the folio size but I/O granularity is smaller. The original condition only hands off folio ownership when the entire folio has been submitted (`*bytes_submitted == folio_len`). Since I/O can be submitted in smaller chunks, this condition fails, leaving `ctx->cur_folio` non-NULL. Consequently, `iomap_read_end` is called again on the folio after the I/O helper already owns it, causing invalid folio access.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
ifs = ifs_alloc(iter->inode, folio, iter->flags);
if (*bytes_submitted == folio_len || !ifs)
    ctx->cur_folio = NULL;
```
Reason:  
Storing the return value of `ifs_alloc` allows checking whether an `ifs` was attached. When `ifs` is `NULL`, the folio has no per-folio state, and the I/O helper is responsible for completing the read regardless of how much was submitted. Adding the `!ifs` condition ensures folio ownership is handed off in that case, preventing a duplicate `iomap_read_end` call and invalid folio access.

